#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231895} :activity/id #uuid "9064539b-24fc-5d46-a38d-08655e37a357" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048846 :topic/id #uuid "c59fe2ed-0027-529f-b6e0-3d5e566657df" :topic/math-academy-id 6769} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232014 :tutorial/content "So far, we have considered the area of a *right triangle*.\nBut what is the area of a *general* triangle, one that is not necessarily a right triangle?\n\nIt turns out that the same idea still works.\n\n> A formula for the area of a triangle is\n> $\\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h$,\n> where $b$ is the base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nThis formula works for *any* triangle.\n\nLet's use this formula to find the area of the triangle below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7c8eaf780eaa0559e8ac4dc2557a423b805705bfd3a081635f5284502896168.png)\n\nIn our triangle,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{base} = 7 \\: \\text{ft}, \\qquad \\textrm{height} = 3 \\: \\text{ft}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 7 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 21 \\\\\n&= 10.5ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the end of this lesson, we'll show how to prove the formula for the area of a triangle.\n\nLet’s see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "35bf275f-a5b4-5e22-8a11-a01a18b1bd80" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14876 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "20b0a099-6ca9-5ba0-b3a5-942ce21c5dd3" :step/math-academy-id 632546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231464 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231397 :question/id #uuid "5821763d-6de9-557f-9688-a690b09082ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-2634"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4c3c332-b81e-5dc9-a031-2d5635bd2e24" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049249}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231765}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Base and Height as Whole Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "88098e77-a8d8-51f6-9125-96ff2b4eb4d0" :step/math-academy-id 632547 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231465 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231398 :question/id #uuid "6a25b580-e4dc-51c4-b807-87810e4a5deb" :question/math-academy-id "e-2636"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ed43817-53c7-5896-9848-73cb2209c39d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231768}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "c34b726a-fd07-5d01-a3ce-1006d2aca94b" :step/math-academy-id 632550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231466 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231399 :question/id #uuid "72173b80-8f7e-5e3f-8185-974455c90509" :question/math-academy-id "e-2637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68d79c4f-277f-5010-a99b-3e1d99b4d433" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048523}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231769}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Base of a Triangle Given the Height and Area"} :step/id #uuid "fe5c0cda-3d44-5857-87a2-559959c7d8a2" :step/math-academy-id 632553 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232013 :tutorial/content "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand this, consider the triangle below, with base $b$ and height $h$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0fa27c8446b799668c5493fa2049eb10550134724dc5e54aab8628a33857f0e.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around it, as follows:\n\n![](images/aa/aa8ae2b0c13e0710a8316c920a4d62e0fe822095311d11f4ca3381f022585725.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nThe area of the enclosing rectangle is $b \\cdot h$. Since the triangle occupies exactly half of this area, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8b463fce-69c3-5e1f-b5c1-15b44aaf6f50" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14875 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Formula For the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "d3617bdf-e6ac-5e17-865e-8d3291845e2e" :step/math-academy-id 632556}] :activity/title "Areas of Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231547}]}
